1
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:23,700
 Stars, galaxies, in astral seas above, on cosmic paths we'll trace.

2
00:00:24,020 --> 00:01:32,980
 Man, we're wings, is such a wondrous thing.

3
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:40,100
 Be high, doves of rum, see, come take your place.

4
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:55,530
 We're leaving soon for...

5
00:01:55,530 --> 00:02:12,230
 Just that you continue your fascinating story after class.

6
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:18,290
 Now, now, silence, silence, silence, my young friend.

7
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:30,840
 Isolation, Andromeda, which we can reach in a few days, is considered by terrestrials to be at a distance of two million light years from their planet.

8
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,020
 Hence, they observe it as it was at the time of the...

9
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:42,060
 What is it?

10
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:43,780
 Come in.

11
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:46,650
 Expecting you, gentlemen.

12
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:47,640
 Commander?

13
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:49,600
 Here is our report.

14
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:53,510
 I'm sure that some parts of it will appear very strange.

15
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,800
 Strange? What do you mean?

16
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,420
 Let's look at two things, Colonel.

17
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:58,990
 Consider the facts.

18
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:03,520
 Their ship has a sudden breakdown, just as it's entering the world of Pegasus.

19
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:05,580
 Yes, well...

20
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:07,580
 Don't you think that's a little strange?

21
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,380
 It's the first time that ever happened.

22
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:13,690
 And the second thing, the planet Lago, according to our information, has no life on it.

23
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:16,160
 Now the captain will tell you what he thinks.

24
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,720
 It's a mystery, I'll admit that.

25
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,510
 This sort of breakdown is pretty rare.

26
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:23,630
 You might think it wasn't accidental.

27
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,520
 Colonel, I think you'd better hear the whole story from the start.

28
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:39,510
 Just routine. Not too boring?

29
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:41,780
 Pegasus system now in visual field.

30
00:03:42,570 --> 00:03:45,210
 In 15 seconds, we cross space border.

31
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:55,280
 I heard that General Grouchley doesn't like anyone to come here.

32
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:57,500
 Don't worry, he'll get used to it.

33
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:08,680
 Stabilization circuits attempting to make compensating corrections to your station

34
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:18,760
 Press it better not go on much longer

35
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:39,070
 Otherwise facing source of fault made it repaired negative extensive suggest manual repair immediately hurry go and repair it

36
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:47,220
 Go ahead go down and take a look in on present trajectory in six minutes

37
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:55,170
 We are in gravitational field planet X 47 logo hurry up somebody go down and help him

38
00:04:55,170 --> 00:05:06,080
 We had a chief mechanic on board who was qualified

39
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:09,680
 He carried out emergency repairs and was able to restore all circuits.

40
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:12,190
 A few seconds more and we'd have disintegrated.

41
00:05:12,530 --> 00:05:14,200
 Done an investigation, I suppose.

42
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:17,760
 It was difficult because many of the cables melted during this incident.

43
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:20,480
 We can't tell what happened, sabotage or accidental.

44
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:22,020
 Personally, I'm not able to say.

45
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:22,460
 I see.

46
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:25,970
 And you were approaching Lago.

47
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:26,770
 Yes, sir.

48
00:05:27,070 --> 00:05:28,100
 We took some pictures.

49
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:32,940
 The color is mainly green and I believe there's some kind of atmosphere that can support a life system.

50
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:35,650
 There's an ocean and some large lakes to support vegetation.

51
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:42,210
 The InfraVision never showed up any trace of a heat source, no laser radar echo, no radio signals at all.

52
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:46,760
 Yes, you're right. It doesn't make sense. It's a contradiction.

53
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:48,770
 Thank you, Captain.

54
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:55,290
 What do you think?

55
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:56,740
 Same as you. I don't understand.

56
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,350
 Let's go and see Perrette. Be her decision what to do.

57
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:04,870
 Atmosphere. Therefore, it can support life.

58
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:12,170
 Dense jungle.

59
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:15,510
 And obviously supporting life.

60
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:29,490
 This proves the report sent in by General Grashley is completely wrong.

61
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:31,650
 He appears to be deceiving us deliberately.

62
00:06:32,170 --> 00:06:33,230
 What do you want?

63
00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:38,480
 That's easy. I'll tell you. He wants a fight. What else would he want?

64
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:42,200
 Well, we'd better put things straight. I propose we see what it's all about.

65
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:43,170
 Good idea.

66
00:06:46,330 --> 00:06:49,220
 More interruptions.

67
00:06:50,050 --> 00:06:51,160
 Yes, who is it?

68
00:06:53,870 --> 00:06:54,270
 Hello?

69
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,790
 Look here, this is not the right time. I'm very busy right now.

70
00:06:57,990 --> 00:06:59,950
 You said that we could see your android, maestro.

71
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:01,780
 Well, uh, hmm.

72
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:06,470
 Yes, what you say you will do, you must do, metro.

73
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:10,410
 Hear the way that he pronounced your name?

74
00:07:10,530 --> 00:07:11,970
 He called you metro.

75
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:13,460
 Hmm.

76
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:16,660
 He must have a wire that's come undone.

77
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:18,980
 and I'll just have a little peek inside and see what it is.

78
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:21,840
 No, Maestro, let him stay that way.

79
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,320
 Metro sounds just fine.

80
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:27,540
 The android's name is Metro.

81
00:07:27,930 --> 00:07:29,370
 Metro, well, that's a funny name.

82
00:07:29,530 --> 00:07:30,550
 How do you like it?

83
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:33,370
 I think it's splendid.

84
00:07:35,130 --> 00:07:36,480
 Metro, what can you do?

85
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:38,540
 I can do everything, Master Ken,

86
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:41,000
 but naturally I am a lot less muddle-headed.

87
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:43,260
 What's that, I'm muddle-headed?

88
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:45,500
 Let's not get off on that subject again.

89
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:48,000
 You know very well it will lead nowhere.

90
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:49,470
 He's a great robot.

91
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:52,810
 You suppose you could make me one that's exactly like him, exactly like him?

92
00:07:52,870 --> 00:07:53,070
 What?

93
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:59,710
 In that case, you will allow me to give you a word of advice.

94
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:03,370
 Select a model without a beard. A long beard is ridiculous.

95
00:08:05,610 --> 00:08:09,600
 Maestro Pierrot, the commander, report to the colonel's office immediately.

96
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:13,830
 You're going to be assigned to a mission. Be careful.

97
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:17,580
 There's something that tells me these unknown worlds are dangerous.

98
00:08:18,890 --> 00:08:20,940
 Anyway, we're going to be together.

99
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:25,920
 The only word for it is strange.

100
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:29,820
 Very well. Here is a detailed explanation of your exact mission.

101
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:32,680
 Listen carefully, you youngsters. Objective, Planet Lago.

102
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:40,400
 Reconnaissance mission following standard procedure.

103
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:44,360
 First, gather all data available concerning inhabitants of any sort.

104
00:08:45,330 --> 00:08:48,240
 Second, discover if there's a civilization and make contact.

105
00:08:49,070 --> 00:08:52,750
 And third, if its level is sufficiently advanced,

106
00:08:53,090 --> 00:08:56,210
 report on the possibility of incorporating it into our confederation.

107
00:08:57,290 --> 00:09:01,280
 Craft will be a light cruiser, fully armed, crew of six.

108
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:04,120
 Father, will I command it to our ship?

109
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:07,430
 Light cruiser?

110
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:08,710
 Know what that means?

111
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:11,410
 No, Gero, not this time.

112
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:13,020
 But Dad, why not?

113
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:14,960
 You said I could.

114
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:18,530
 Lieutenant, here, I am the one in charge.

115
00:09:19,260 --> 00:09:23,490
 Now then, there is some mystery hidden in this business, a danger.

116
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:24,070
 Yes, Colonel?

117
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:28,020
 That's why I am putting the commander in charge.

118
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:30,650
 What is it?

119
00:09:31,590 --> 00:09:33,540
 What about that we land?

120
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:33,820
 Yes.

121
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:34,160
 Okay.

122
00:09:34,910 --> 00:09:39,770
 The commander suggests once you are on the planet, there is no reason why you can't take command, Lieutenant.

123
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:40,150
 Yes, sir.

124
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:43,910
 At that point, your superior will be no more than an observer and counselor.

125
00:09:44,930 --> 00:09:47,810
 As for you, Maestro, you're a bit too slow.

126
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:49,170
 Where is your android?

127
00:09:49,170 --> 00:09:51,790
 I believe it was supposed to be ready for trials.

128
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:54,570
 Well, all work and no play, as they say.

129
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:02,870
 Well, look here, young man.

130
00:10:02,990 --> 00:10:03,970
 I wouldn't laugh.

131
00:10:04,050 --> 00:10:07,530
 You can be replaced one day by one of my little robots.

132
00:10:07,710 --> 00:10:08,330
 Take him along.

133
00:10:08,510 --> 00:10:09,730
 You will see what I mean.

134
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:52,020
 You'll assist me in the descent.

135
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:54,030
 You see a sort of plateau there?

136
00:10:54,070 --> 00:10:56,150
 It will serve as a good observation center.

137
00:10:56,510 --> 00:10:58,190
 I'll remain in command of the ship,

138
00:10:58,190 --> 00:11:01,030
 but from now on, you will be in charge of the expedition.

139
00:11:02,350 --> 00:11:03,050
 Attention, crew.

140
00:11:03,350 --> 00:11:05,270
 This is Lieutenant Pierrot calling the crew.

141
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:08,300
 As soon as we land, the second lieutenant and myself

142
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:14,270
 will set off on our reconnaissance mission the others stay on board awaiting orders from our

143
00:11:14,270 --> 00:11:19,930
 commander two will sleep the third keeps watch surveying the terrain the last will stay in radio

144
00:11:19,930 --> 00:11:25,050
 contact if we should request assistance or if no contact for three hours a search and rescue unit

145
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:30,800
 will be sent out by the commander they're landing chief i could see that for myself idiot what are

146
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:44,570
 your honors chief weapons in firing positions wait for my orders ready to fire time see that

147
00:11:44,570 --> 00:11:47,010
 What's that big hunk of machinery there?

148
00:11:47,010 --> 00:11:49,630
 Let 'em come closer, they're fug to watch.

149
00:11:49,630 --> 00:11:54,780
 Bugs, aren't they?

150
00:11:54,780 --> 00:11:59,040
 We can eat them.

151
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:04,380
 The ugliest things I ever saw, yeah.

152
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:08,820
 Worse than that, they're disgusting.

153
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:15,440
 They walk on two little tails, huh?

154
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,110
 There are only two of them.

155
00:12:17,110 --> 00:12:18,880
 Can't be too dangerous.

156
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:21,350
 Hey, hey, hey, wait, wait, wait, what about me?

157
00:12:21,350 --> 00:12:23,260
 Hey, hey, hey!

158
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:30,680
 Leave that one.

159
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,530
 What is it, walking artichoke or a weapon?

160
00:12:33,530 --> 00:12:37,180
 Better warn the council.

161
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:39,860
 So, you'd just leave me in a closet, would you?

162
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:45,780
 That's very intelligent. I take all the trouble of coming here, and you just let me rust away on the landing craft, really.

163
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:49,190
 He fusses as much as Maestro. You think his complaining is built in?

164
00:12:49,430 --> 00:12:51,580
 Okay, no distractions. It's unknown territory.

165
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:56,220
 You stay ten paces back and weapon in hand, ready to go into action no matter what.

166
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:58,560
 Oh, sorry.

167
00:12:58,930 --> 00:13:02,040
 Yeah, okay, let's go. We'll start off that way.

168
00:13:05,090 --> 00:13:08,710
 Don't know why, but it's hard to breathe.

169
00:13:09,050 --> 00:13:11,690
 The atmosphere is rich in carbon dioxide. It accelerates the pulse.

170
00:13:11,710 --> 00:13:15,550
 By the way, maybe you'll tell me whether the planet we're exploring is inhabited.

171
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:16,860
 An impossibly stupid question.

172
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:18,820
 How am I supposed to reply when I have insufficient information?

173
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:20,180
 What am I clairvoyant or...

174
00:13:20,180 --> 00:13:54,300
 They're so ugly.

175
00:13:56,150 --> 00:13:58,750
 Or contain some very strange species.

176
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:01,430
 Species? That's easy to say.

177
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:03,260
 But what are they? I think they're insects.

178
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:06,650
 Really stupid nonsense, my brothers.

179
00:14:06,890 --> 00:14:08,430
 These are intelligent creatures.

180
00:14:08,890 --> 00:14:10,630
 Why do you say that, Father Ratloss?

181
00:14:10,890 --> 00:14:11,870
 Do they look like us?

182
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:13,030
 No, they're horrible.

183
00:14:13,410 --> 00:14:14,450
 You can say that again.

184
00:14:14,590 --> 00:14:15,200
 They're horrible.

185
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:19,120
 It follows that there is a world where there are species different from us.

186
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:19,600
 Agreed?

187
00:14:19,930 --> 00:14:20,780
 Do you agree?

188
00:14:21,470 --> 00:14:24,900
 To build a machine like that, they must have some intelligence.

189
00:14:25,970 --> 00:14:29,560
 So let us make contact with these repulsive foreigners.

190
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:31,020
 Perhaps it's in our interest.

191
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:33,130
 In any case, there is always time to take action.

192
00:14:33,130 --> 00:14:34,850
 Yes, at least we can meet them.

193
00:14:35,070 --> 00:14:35,770
 He's right.

194
00:14:36,710 --> 00:14:38,190
 For the crocodiles?

195
00:14:38,740 --> 00:14:39,830
 Let them come.

196
00:14:40,150 --> 00:14:42,050
 After all, what can they do to us?

197
00:14:42,430 --> 00:14:43,390
 Yes, why not?

198
00:14:43,510 --> 00:14:44,320
 Let them come.

199
00:14:45,130 --> 00:14:46,530
 They might taste delicious.

200
00:14:48,210 --> 00:14:51,250
 Come, come, Theodol. Keep calm.

201
00:14:51,250 --> 00:15:04,850
 Still on the path.

202
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:06,940
 Just one second.

203
00:15:07,710 --> 00:15:13,130
 Vegetation exactly conforms to that of the Triassic period, 200 million years ago.

204
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:14,690
 You're a regular genius.

205
00:15:35,460 --> 00:15:45,420
 Excited, quite right.

206
00:15:46,230 --> 00:15:47,710
 We bid you all welcome.

207
00:15:48,660 --> 00:15:50,590
 Yes, well, to our land.

208
00:15:53,140 --> 00:15:53,740
 The stanzas.

209
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:56,000
 We have come here in peace.

210
00:15:58,370 --> 00:16:00,390
 Ridiculous gurgling sound it makes.

211
00:16:00,630 --> 00:16:02,290
 Wait, I'll find the old wise one.

212
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:02,950
 He knows everything.

213
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:08,260
 Kind of nice in a way, these pet lizards.

214
00:16:08,380 --> 00:16:09,500
 Too bad we can't understand them.

215
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:14,560
 Descendants from the Saltoparkas, originating as crocodiles, dinosaurs, and winged reptiles.

216
00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:16,600
 Circulatory system, top side.

217
00:16:16,970 --> 00:16:19,990
 They do not seem hostile, appears they would like to make contact.

218
00:16:20,190 --> 00:16:20,750
 End of report.

219
00:16:21,010 --> 00:16:24,310
 I'll tell you, if they're all like this one, I imagine they're pretty powerful.

220
00:16:24,630 --> 00:16:26,190
 Trigger finger ready and eyes open.

221
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:28,410
 You too, don't go to sleep, okay?

222
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,980
 I don't sleep. What should I do?

223
00:16:31,650 --> 00:16:36,780
 I want to tell you that we are here as ambassadors of peace from another world.

224
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:40,240
 I understand you. We wish to welcome you here in peace.

225
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:41,370
 Huh?

226
00:16:41,870 --> 00:16:48,600
 Oh, that's right, you aren't receiving me. Wait, try.

227
00:16:48,770 --> 00:16:54,920
 Listen, now you can understand. Welcome to here.

228
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:56,920
 Well, I never saw that talking lizard.

229
00:16:57,430 --> 00:16:58,220
 Why not?

230
00:16:58,460 --> 00:16:59,420
 Now let us talk.

231
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:03,610
 If you will follow us to our city, we will be very honored.

232
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,350
 Friends look worried.

233
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:12,190
 Are they scared?

234
00:17:12,730 --> 00:17:14,280
 No, they don't talk much.

235
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:15,280
 Guess they're a bit shy.

236
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:16,880
 Oh, yes, I understand.

237
00:17:17,190 --> 00:17:18,530
 Each to his own ways.

238
00:17:18,940 --> 00:17:20,010
 That's how it is.

239
00:17:23,170 --> 00:17:28,210
 You need to receive your language to understand it.

240
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:34,690
 How do you explain the fact that a people who can construct a flying machine with all its marvels cannot communicate through telepathy?

241
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:37,740
 Oh, it's a very special gift you have.

242
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:39,360
 Ah, yes.

243
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:40,740
 Just as I thought.

244
00:17:40,980 --> 00:17:48,340
 Mm-hmm. They're more like Megalobatharachis rather than the planobracatavitkia.

245
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:49,780
 A clever fellow.

246
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:52,340
 Yes, he is. He's the smartest among us.

247
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:55,100
 I wouldn't say we're much like him.

248
00:17:56,060 --> 00:17:59,250
 No. Back home, we call him an intelligent robot.

249
00:18:01,420 --> 00:18:06,020
 That little skinny one tastes good.

250
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:12,410
 I've got my eye on that one over there. Lots of meat on that one.

251
00:18:17,860 --> 00:18:21,130
 A little more respect for our honored guests.

252
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:23,200
 Come and see our sports field.

253
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:25,810
 We're playing the tail-bashing championship.

254
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:26,380
 Take a look.

255
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:32,740
 Can I ask you, I don't understand something.

256
00:18:32,860 --> 00:18:37,040
 How is it your females lay something like 50 or more eggs, but each family has only two children?

257
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:38,740
 How do you manage to arrange that?

258
00:18:39,230 --> 00:18:41,750
 Three for especially meritorious families.

259
00:18:42,910 --> 00:18:43,470
 Enough, isn't it?

260
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:46,840
 Yes, but, well, where are the others?

261
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:48,720
 Why, we eat them.

262
00:18:49,070 --> 00:18:50,000
 Quite delicious.

263
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:52,700
 A truly tasty dish.

264
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:57,270
 If you are still here when they're in season, you will taste some.

265
00:18:57,270 --> 00:19:00,330
 Oh, yes, but, well, they're your own children.

266
00:19:00,550 --> 00:19:01,890
 Oh, no, not quite.

267
00:19:02,290 --> 00:19:02,890
 Not yet.

268
00:19:03,210 --> 00:19:04,970
 And besides, what would you have us do?

269
00:19:06,370 --> 00:19:11,250
 Ensure that. You know the formula. There are 4 billion of us on the planet.

270
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:18,300
 If you multiply that figure by 50 every year, what is the total that you end up?

271
00:19:19,090 --> 00:19:23,820
 In one year, exactly 200 billion. Exactly in two years, 10,000 billion.

272
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:26,780
 In three years, 50,000 billion, etc.

273
00:19:34,890 --> 00:19:49,090
 It's easy. The first thing to do is clear the field so you can tail the ball into your opponent's goal.

274
00:19:52,450 --> 00:19:56,580
 Let's go, lizards! Let's go, lizards! Let's go!

275
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:06,480
 Very nice, kind, practically civilized.

276
00:20:07,030 --> 00:20:10,810
 Could they join our confederation? Not like us at all.

277
00:20:12,250 --> 00:20:13,930
 Let's go, lizards.

278
00:20:14,300 --> 00:20:18,550
 They have no masters or slaves or cast or races, in my opinion. Positive.

279
00:20:19,090 --> 00:20:22,130
 On the other hand, they hold no beliefs, have no language or art.

280
00:20:22,250 --> 00:20:24,480
 As for their philosophy, it's quite elementary.

281
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:27,840
 Yeah, well, all the same, I mean, soccer, we can't beat them, but it's true.

282
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:30,160
 They don't always distinguish between the opponent and the ball.

283
00:20:30,950 --> 00:20:33,680
 Didn't happen to run into General Grouchley.

284
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:37,980
 No, no sign of him. We talked about a great many things.

285
00:20:40,010 --> 00:20:43,050
 The one thing that bothers me is that they still reproduce by laying eggs.

286
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:48,440
 And the fact that they eat their surplus, it's well like cannibalism.

287
00:20:49,390 --> 00:20:54,500
 Look, Commander, why not come with us tomorrow? I understand that their Grand Council will be in session. You can judge.

288
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:57,360
 I told you, Lieutenant, you're in charge. I follow your orders.

289
00:20:59,430 --> 00:21:05,210
 I've been given to understand that your species has never known war.

290
00:21:05,210 --> 00:21:10,590
 Ah, war. Well, let me think. The old legends speak of our having shared the planet with some other creatures.

291
00:21:10,830 --> 00:21:12,730
 They were a distant relative.

292
00:21:12,730 --> 00:21:15,440
 Crocodile lizards. Cousins, I suppose.

293
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:17,660
 They were stupid creatures.

294
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:22,470
 Aggressive, dangerous.

295
00:21:22,470 --> 00:21:24,410
 They used to come and share our eggs.

296
00:21:24,410 --> 00:21:26,940
 They were enough for everyone.

297
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:28,940
 But then, one day, they started to attack our adults,

298
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:30,990
 our women, and children.

299
00:21:30,990 --> 00:21:33,100
 Naturally, we had to put a stop to that.

300
00:21:33,100 --> 00:21:37,680
 And that meant war.

301
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:43,040
 War.

302
00:21:52,840 --> 00:22:02,970
 We revised weapons against them,

303
00:22:02,970 --> 00:22:05,180
 and one by one, all of them were exterminated.

304
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:11,030
 exterminated i get the impression that shocks you would have been a mistake to show any leniency

305
00:22:11,030 --> 00:22:16,280
 extermination was the best way to keep them from continuing the war because now we have peace as

306
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:21,690
 we want it and other men like us have you ever seen anyone like us or are we the first ones you

307
00:22:21,690 --> 00:22:29,420
 are the first but we knew you would come one day three times now we have seen machines like yours

308
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:38,260
 it's the general huh the cassiopeians this new one's even more appetizing than the others right

309
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:40,960
 You are, but the elders aren't going to like it.

310
00:22:41,800 --> 00:22:45,210
 Old fogies, all they do is grumble.

311
00:22:46,020 --> 00:22:50,940
 Meanwhile, they know that us kids have to have a little fun every once in a while.

312
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:55,590
 Well, naturally, there's a social code governing your way of life.

313
00:22:55,880 --> 00:22:56,560
 Any laws?

314
00:22:57,230 --> 00:22:59,370
 I don't know what good laws would be.

315
00:23:00,110 --> 00:23:01,580
 We don't need them here at all.

316
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:02,880
 We don't bother with them.

317
00:23:03,770 --> 00:23:06,490
 Everybody does what he wants according to his instinct,

318
00:23:06,750 --> 00:23:08,730
 so long as he doesn't get in anyone else's way.

319
00:23:08,730 --> 00:23:10,350
 Of course, the young ones.

320
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:14,830
 They can be impetuous sometimes, but you know what it's like to be young.

321
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:22,400
 Watch out! Watch out!

322
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:38,810
 Let's go!

323
00:23:40,030 --> 00:23:44,000
 Watch out! They're right behind us! Turn and fire!

324
00:23:48,250 --> 00:23:55,220
 Hey there!

325
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:14,480
 Who's not with us? What's he doing?

326
00:24:15,380 --> 00:24:16,350
 Wait for me!

327
00:24:17,820 --> 00:24:19,120
 Well, Metro, late as usual.

328
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:21,760
 Doing the best I can, and you're not funny.

329
00:24:21,960 --> 00:24:25,080
 I'd like to see you with arrows bulking up all your circuits.

330
00:24:25,220 --> 00:24:26,760
 I just might blow a fuse or something.

331
00:24:33,690 --> 00:24:40,570
 Congratulations.

332
00:24:40,570 --> 00:24:41,920
 Oh, you did a good job.

333
00:24:42,660 --> 00:24:45,260
 They can get rough, those friends of ours, when they want to, right?

334
00:24:45,630 --> 00:24:49,680
 How were they to know that our paralyzers would only knock them out for an hour?

335
00:24:50,020 --> 00:24:52,100
 By now, they should be coming, too.

336
00:24:52,760 --> 00:24:54,720
 We have a little punch-up at the end, anyway.

337
00:24:55,100 --> 00:24:56,350
 Too impulsive, my son.

338
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:59,190
 It would appear that I alone was wounded.

339
00:24:59,840 --> 00:25:01,090
 Hardly seems right.

340
00:25:01,630 --> 00:25:02,490
 What a character.

341
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:05,010
 Don't worry, Maestro will put you together again.

342
00:25:05,010 --> 00:25:06,170
 You'll be as good as new.

